## 2. GGUF Split Path Resolution: Symlink Handling

**Component:** `include/strata/artifact/gguf_split.hpp` — `gguf_split_paths()`

**Responsibilities:** Resolves any shard path to the full ordered shard list by rewriting the 5-digit shard number. Throws when a shard is missing.

**Change:** Hugging Face snapshot directories use symlinks whose *names* are the split filenames but whose *targets* are hash-named blobs. `std::ifstream` follows symlinks, so the existence check works, but the returned path is the symlink name (correct for opening). However, if a user passes the hash-named blob target directly (a common mistake when scripting `huggingface-cli download --local-dir-use-symlinks False` vs `True`), the regex does not match and the function returns a single-element vector — silently treating one blob as the whole model. Add a diagnostic: when the path does not match the split pattern but a sibling with the `-00001-of-` pattern exists in the same directory, warn and resolve through the sibling.

```diff
--- a/include/strata/artifact/gguf_split.hpp
+++ b/include/strata/artifact/gguf_split.hpp
@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@
 #pragma once

 #include <cstdio>
+#include <filesystem>
 #include <fstream>
 #include <stdexcept>
 #include <string>
@@ -30,6 +31,22 @@ inline std::vector<std::string> gguf_split_paths(const std::string& any) {
         if (a < '0' || a > '9' || b < '0' || b > '9') return {any};
         no = no * 10 + (a - '0');
         n = n * 10 + (b - '0');
     }
+    // A path that does not match the split pattern might be a hash-named blob target from a
+    // Hugging Face snapshot.  If a sibling with the split naming exists, resolve through it.
+    if (at == std::string::npos || at < 6 || any[at - 6] != '-') {
+        namespace fs = std::filesystem;
+        const fs::path p(any);
+        if (p.has_parent_path()) {
+            for (const auto& e : fs::directory_iterator(p.parent_path())) {
+                const std::string name = e.path().filename().string();
+                if (name.size() > 20 && name.find("-00001-of-") != std::string::npos &&
+                    name.size() >= 10 && name.compare(name.size() - 5, 5, ".gguf") == 0) {
+                    return gguf_split_paths(e.path().string());
+                }
+            }
+        }
+        return {any};
+    }
     if (n < 1 || no < 1 || no > n) return {any};
     std::vector<std::string> out;
     for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
```

**Verification cases:**
1. A directory with `model-00001-of-00002.gguf` → symlink to `abc123.gguf` and `model-00002-of-00002.gguf` → symlink to `def456.gguf`. Passing `abc123.gguf` resolves to the two split paths.
2. A single-file model `model.gguf` with no siblings matching the pattern: returns `{any}` unchanged.
3. A directory with two different split families (`a-00001-of-00002.gguf` and `b-00001-of-00003.gguf`): the function picks the first found; a test asserts it does not crash and returns a valid shard list.

